tei:div.p:
The divine youth Mañjuśrī then got up from his seat 
                         and circumambulated the thus-gone lord Saṃkusumita Rājendra
                        three times. Then, bowing his head and placing his right knee on the ground, he spoke to Lord Saṃkusumita Rājendra: {1.7}

“Good, good it is, O son of the Victorious One, that you have entered this particular samādhi! No śrāvakas,
                        pratyeka­buddhas, or bodhisattvas who are established in the perfect conduct, not even those on the tenth bodhisattva level,
                        are able to enter this samādhi.” {1.15}

lg.l:
Who accomplishes all purposes of [mantra]
                                Mantracaryā (mantra conduct) is a recurrent phrase in this text, used
                                about eighty-nine times. Even if “mantra” is not mentioned explicitly, the “conduct” (caryā) implies “mantra conduct” (mantracaryā), especially when talking about its purpose (artha). conduct! {1.23}

tei:div.p:
The tathāgata Jyotis­saumya­gandhāvabhāsa­śrī, the tathāgata Bhaiṣajya­guru­vaiḍūrya­prabha­rāja, the tathāgata
                        Samantāvabhāsa­śrī, the tathāgata Samudgata­rāja, the tathāgata Śālendra­rāja, the tathāgata Lokendra­rāja, the tathāgata
                        Amitāyurjñāna­viniścaya­rāja, the tathāgata Anantāvabhāsa­rājendra, and the tathāgata Jyoti­raśmirājendra. {1.36}

Also the great dūtīs, the messengers of Lord Vajrapāṇi, together with their retinues of thousands of millions of
                        vidyārājñīs, abiding nonconceptually in their own sphere of phenomena that has the nature of the sky, gathered in that place.
                        They were: {1.55}

These and other great dūtīs, surrounded by retinues of many other dūtīs, were all gathered in that great assembly.
                        There were also many dhāraṇī goddesses who arise in the mind absorbed in samādhi, and who let their chastising sticks fall
                        upon wicked beings to control them. They were: {1.57}

Lord Śākyamuni then looked at this entire gathering and, knowing with his pure heart that the realm of beings
                            chos kyi dbyings Tib. extends beyond the three times and is
                        sky-like in nature, without independent existence, he addressed Mañjuśrī, the divine youth, as follows: {1.67}

“Homage to all the buddhas who arise out of the nature of nonexistence of individual entities! Homage to the
                        pratyeka­buddhas and the noble śrāvakas! Homage to the bodhisattvas, the lords established on the ten levels, the great
                        beings! This is the mantra proper: {1.70}

Pīlu, Upapīlu, Supīlu, Anantapīlu, Manoratha, Amanoratha, TMK reflects
                            the Sanskrit *Sumanoratha for Amanoratha. Sutāpa, Grasana, Supāna,
                            gzi brjid bzang po Tib. Ghora, and Ghorarūpin. {1.79}

They all gathered to hear the Dharma. Also, the great nāga kings, with
                        retinues of many hundreds of thousands of millions of nāgas, were brought by the
                            Lord of Wrath through his display of the power of the bodhisattva. They were: {1.80}

These and other nāgas, with retinues of many hundreds of thousands of
                        millions of nāgas, gathered together in this great assembly and took their seats to
                        hear the Dharma. Also, the sages and the great sages gathered there. They were: {1.82}

These nakṣatra queens, with retinues of many hundreds of thousands of nakṣatras, gathered together in this great
                        assembly in the realm of the Pure Abode through the blessing of the Buddha and took their seats. Present were also the
                        following thirty-six signs of the zodiac: {1.97}

These great rāśis, with retinues of many hundreds of thousands of rāśis, came to this great gathering in the realm of
                        the Pure Abode. Having arrived, they bowed to the Lord’s feet and sat down in their respective seats. Also, the great
                        yakṣiṇīs, with retinues of many hundreds of thousands of yakṣiṇīs, were present there. They were: {1.99}

These great yakṣiṇīs, with retinues of many hundreds of thousands of yakṣiṇīs, bowing to Lord Śākyamuni from a
                        distance, were present in this great gathering. Also the great piśācīs, with retinues of many hundreds of thousands of
                        piśācīs, joined in, paying respects to Lord Śākyamuni. They were: {1.101}

Now, Lord Śākyamuni, having looked over this entire assembly with his buddha eye, said to Mañjuśrī, the divine youth,
                        “Please speak, O pure being! Give a full presentation of the section on samādhi that explains the meaning of mantra practice.
                        Present this basket of bodhisattva teachings if you think that the moment is right.” {1.107}

Then Mañjuśrī, the divine youth, with the approval of Lord Śākyamuni, entered the samādhi called the ornament of a contiguous formation
solid as fused vajras, adorned with miraculous displays
“Adorned with miraculous displays” is missing from the Tibetan. This reading seems plausible, as it avoids the repetition found in the Skt. of the phrase vyūhālaṅkāraṃ. of the space-like nature. As soon as Mañjuśrī, the divine youth, entered this samādhi, he blessed the realm of the Pure Abode, which extended over many hundreds of thousands of leagues, so that it became made of vajras. All the many yakṣas, rākṣasas, gandharvas, mārutas, and piśācas—in short, the entire universe inhabited by living beings—became situated by the blessing of the bodhisattvas in this great palace that resembled diamond jewels and gems, and there took their seats together, without any mutual enmity. {1.108}

“With three qualities, his exposition of the meaning of mantra practice will be perfect. What are these three? They
                        are (1) never abandoning any sentient being, (2) guarding the vows of the bodhisattva’s discipline, and (3) never abandoning
                        his mantra. {1.113}

“Speech, hūṁ!
                         Skt.: vākye hūṁ.
                        {2.13}

“Oṁ.
                            maM Tib. 
                        {2.15}

“This mantra calls upon Lord Mañjuśrī and also summons all beings—all bodhisattvas, all pratyeka­buddhas, venerable
                        śrāvakas, gods, nāgas, yakṣas, gandharvas,
                        garuḍas, kinnaras, mahoragas, piśācas, rākṣasas, and all spirits. {2.20}

“One should offer, with the above mantra, bali of food that satisfies all the senses. 
                         In this set there also is a mudrā called
                            spear, which wards off all evildoers. Here belongs also the mantra of lamps: {2.32}

“This vidyā, Keśinī by name, is an attendant of Mañjuśrī and
                        may be employed in all rites. When combined with the great five-crested mudrā, she may be employed in all
                        rites involving poison. {2.50}

lg.l:
Her divine form.
                                thams cad bzugs can Tib. 
                            {2.54}

And does not meet with its teaching on the samaya, This line is omitted in
                                the Tib.
                            {2.114}

tei:div.p:
“The blessed Prajñāpāramitā should be read in the south,
the noble Candra­pradīpa­samādhi Also known as the Samādhi­rāja­sūtra. See Peter Alan Roberts, trans., The King of Samādhis Sūtra (Toh 127), 2018. in the west, the noble Gaṇḍavyūha See Peter Alan Roberts, trans., The Stem Array (Toh 44-45), 2021a. in the north, and the noble Suvarṇa­prabhāsottama­sūtra
See Peter Alan Roberts, trans., The Sūtra of the Sublime Golden Light (1) (Toh 555), 2023. in the eastern direction. If the texts are not available, the master should instruct four Dharma reciters, learned in these four sūtras, to recite them accordingly. Then the maṇḍala master, rising up in order to listen to the Dharma, “In order to listen to the Dharma” om. Tib. should strew white flowers of nice fragrance mixed with sandalwood, camphor, and saffron all over the maṇḍala while reciting the root mantra. Having thus bestrewn it, he should exit the maṇḍala. {2.129}

lg.l:
White, yellow, red, dark blue, and green. This passage, versified in Skt.,
                                is rendered as prose in the Tibetan.
                            {2.133}

tei:div.p:
“After bathing, ritually pure and wearing clean clothes, he should go to the fire pit and perform the rite of
                        protection and offer one thousand and eight oblations of ghee mixed with saffron while reciting the root mantra. Subsequently,
                        he should sit down on a bundle of kuśa grass and remain there, reciting. {2.137}

“The sixteen bodhisattvas should also be included: Samantabhadra; Kṣitigarbha; Gaganagañja;
                        Sarva­nīvaraṇa­viṣkambhin; Apāyajaha; Maitreya with yak-tail whisk in his hand and looking at the Blessed Buddha; Vimalamati;
                        Vimalaketu; Sudhana;
                            nor bzang dang / Tib. The Tibetan here seems to be translating
                            *Maṇibhadra, which could simply be a translation choice, as maṇibhadra is
                            close in meaning to sudhana. Candraprabha; Vimalakīrti;
                        Bhaiṣajyarāja; Sarva­dharmīśvara­rāja; Lokagati; Mahāmati; and Patidhara.
                            blo bzang po Tib. The Tibetan reflects the Sanskrit *Sumati. Each of these sixteen great bodhisattvas should be depicted in
                        a peaceful form adorned with all types of jewelry. {2.141}

“In the northwestern division should be the king of yakṣas Dhanada, the king
                        of gandharvas Pañcaśikha, and the king of kinnaras Druma. These three must always be included in the painting. The remaining
                        ones should be represented by their respective mudrās and arranged in sequence according to their respective places. {2.171}

“Outside the third maṇḍala there should be a fourth, comprised of five concentric zones and adorned with rows of
                        mudrās. It has four sides, each including a gateway graced with [one of] the four great kings. The emblems are arranged in the
                        following order: {2.172}

“When all this has been drawn, three mudrās should be traced with colored powders outside the gate of the main
                        maṇḍala: one above, one below, and one at the same level. The three mudrās to be painted are clothes, a fly-whisk, and a pair
                        of shoes, each surrounded by blazing light.” {2.176}

“Consequently, it is the princely youths who should be ushered in first. This will elevate their regal status and
                        promote long life, health, power, and the ability to savor sensual enjoyments. In particular, this will stabilize the
                        accomplishment of mantra for them, the inexperienced. {2.180}

lg.l:
Camphor, saffron, and sandalwood mixed together. This passage is rendered in
                                prose in the Tibetan. 
                            {2.182}

And are required in the case of all mantra deities. It is not clear which
                                procedure this verse refers to.
                            {2.189}

And accomplish whatever you desire.’ This verse and the next are rendered in
                                prose in the Tib.
                            {2.205}

tei:div.p:
“In all rituals such as these, the maṇḍala should be drawn at midnight, or midday, “Midday” om. Tib. or otherwise at any time. Employing this very
                        one-syllable mantra, one should present a welcome offering of flowers to the deity and then dismiss him. Having done so, one
                        should inundate the maṇḍala with water. This will provide strong protection to all those who are weary. {3.8}

lg.l:
As his mind focuses on the thread. The translation of this line is based on
                                the Tibetan.
                            {4.17}

The weaving will be completed quickly. The translation of this verse is
                                uncertain. Both the Skt. and the Tibetan are unclear.
                            {4.44}

tei:div.p:
“First one should paint the Tathāgata Śākyamuni, endowed with all of his excellent forms, his body marked with the
                        thirty-two marks of a perfect man and adorned with the eighty minor characteristics. He sits on a jeweled lotus, his form
                        surrounded by a halo of light to the distance of one fathom all around. He teaches the Dharma with a serene expression. {4.65}

“On the fifth lotus is the noble Mahāmati; on the sixth, Śāntamati; on the seventh, Vairocana­garbha; and on the
                        eighth, Apāyajaha—these bodhisattvas, too, should be depicted. They hold in their hands fruits and books, are adorned with all
                        types of jewelry, and wear an upper garment and short trousers of fine silk. {4.74}

“Two gods from the realm of the Pure Abode should be painted among the clouds above the tathāgatas, each in an upper
                        corner of the canvas. They should be depicted releasing a rain of flowers. Standing in the midst of space, they salute all the
                        buddhas, bodhisattvas, pratyeka­buddhas, and the noble śrāvakas. {4.80}

“Above them should be painted two more gods, near to the other two gods of the Pure Abode. They hold a long, elegant
                        canopy of fine cloth, spreading it above all the buddhas, bodhisattvas, pratyeka­buddhas, and noble śrāvakas. They should be
                        depicted wearing celestial garlands and clothes. {4.83}

“Above the head of Lord Śākyamuni one should paint a bundle of jeweled threads, including strings of pearls, gems,
                        rubies, sapphires, and so forth. On this bundle, there should be a well-arrayed canopy of silk, decorated all around with
                        hanging strings of pearls. {4.84}

“Below this mountain, one should paint the practitioner sitting on a slab of stone, with his knees touching the
                        ground, and with an incense censer in his hand. He holds the emblems consistent with his dress and form and acts in the
                        attitude of obedience. He should be depicted with his gaze directed at Noble Mañjuśrī. {4.87}

“There, on the jeweled peak made of beryl, completely enclosed and shaded by a pannay tree whose branches are all
                        covered in flowers with buds fully open, is the blessed Tārā. The tree’s overspread branches are bent down with fresh sprouts,
                        in a variety of shapes, bright and colorful. The goddess Tārā should be painted looking ahead. {4.90}

lg.l:
Situated at the top of his mountain, His mountain, described a few
                                paragraphs above, is situated below the lotus seat of Śākyamuni and at the feet of Mañjuśrī.
                            {4.94}

tei:div.p:
“First one should paint the realm of the Pure Abode, its form beautiful in every respect, abounding in things made of
                        jewels, and adorned with garlands of white pearls. Portrayed in the middle should be Lord Śākyamuni, sitting on the lion
                        throne expounding the Dharma and excellent in every aspect. {5.3}

“To the right of the Blessed One and near Noble Mañjuśrī should be depicted the great assembly. The eight great
                        śrāvakas and eight pratyeka­buddhas should be painted exactly as before, except that the noble Mahā­maudgalyāyana and
                        Śāriputra are standing up and fanning Lord Śākyamuni with a yak-tail whisk. {5.7}

“The gods from the realm of the Pure Abode, Śakra, the lord of gods, and Suyāma, as well as the gods
                        from the heavens of Tuṣita, Sunirmita, Śuddha, Vimala, Sudṛśa, Atapas, and Ābhāsvara should also be included. In addition,
                        Brahmā Sahāmpati and the gods from the Akaniṣṭha heaven should be included. These and other gods who dwell in the
                        realm of form and the realm of desire should be painted arranged in sequence, near to Noble Mañjuśrī. They should be painted
                        arranged in tiers above the assembly with their respective features and dress. {5.8}

“Following the procedures as previously described, skilled craftsmen should prepare a cloth one width of the Buddha’s
                        hand across that is in the same four-sided shape as before. The painting should then be done with the paints as previously
                        described. {6.2}

lg.l:
The mantra deities “Mantra deities” om. Tib. will be
                                accomplished. In the Tib., this passage is in prose, not verse.
                            {7.24}

tei:div.p:
“If she experiences bleeding within the next three to five years when she is with child; if she does not conceive a
                        child for many years;
                            lo gsum mam lo lnga’i bar du bu btsa’ ba’i dus su khrag ’byung ba’am/ lo du ma’i
                                bar du bu ma chags pa’am/ D The translation of these lines is based on the Tibetan, as the Skt. is not
                            clear. if she is harmed by the mantra magic, medicines, or mudrās of others; This suggests non-Buddhist mantras. if she suffers a miscarriage; if she falls ill;
                        if there is any other disorder caused by others; or if she is poisoned by animate or inanimate, or natural or artificial
                        means—if any of this was effected by the friendly or hostile use of any root or medicine mantras, one should chant the mantra
                        twenty-seven times and then grind old ghee with the moon-like eye of a peacock feather, making it homogenous. When it is
                        finely ground, one should mix it with sugar and eat an amount the size of myrobalan fruit every day over seven days. {9.10}

“With the lighted lamps evenly arranged in contiguous rows, light will radiate from the painting. The moment the
                        light shines, the painting will be haloed with a blazing ring of light that pervades everywhere. In the sky above drums will
                        sound and congratulatory cheers will be heard. {9.17}

lg.l:
In the country of Saṃkaśya,
                                nai ka sha dang cho ’phrul bstan/ D; ne ka sha dang cho ’phrul bstan/ N. The Tibetan identifies the place as *Naikaśa or
                                *Nekaśa. the place of great miracles; {10.23}

tei:div.p:
“There is, Mañjuśrī, in your ritual, a painting procedure of the medium type, a practice procedure serving as the
                        means for accomplishing middling rites. I will teach it in brief, so please listen carefully and reflect upon it well. I will
                        teach it now.” {11.1}

lg.l:
Is four angular cubits Four angular cubits (or one angular yoke), as an
                                astronomical distance between celestial objects, is the angular distance of eight degrees (if the Indian astronomical
                                angular measures of cubit and yoke corresponded to those used in ancient Babylon). above the horizon, {11.65}

He should place his right hand
                                lag pa g.yas pas byin pa g.yas/ Tib. “His right hand” has been
                                supplied from the Tibetan. on his right calf {11.104}

Then second, the maṇḍala of the lord of one’s own mantra;
                                rang sngags Tib. This translation reflects the Tibetan, which
                                reflects the Sanskrit *svamantra instead of the extant Skt. sumantra.

And as powerful as a drum or a thunderclap, “Thunderclap” om.
                                Tib.
                            {11.145}

tei:div.p:
“When one has accomplished the mantra taught by Lord Kāśyapa and achieved the goal of removing hunger and thirst, one
                        should practice the teachings on the method for accomplishing the goal of mantra activity, strictly following the procedure
                        and using the specified implements. Otherwise one will not succeed. {11.160}

“In brief, one should follow the mantra practice step by step in this way, and one will certainly succeed. “And one will certainly succeed” om. Tib. One will
                        definitely obtain all the power substances, special implements, and medicines, as well as jewels and gems as previously
                        described. {11.161}

“One should then visualize oneself and everything illumined by the light issuing forth from the tuft of hair between
                        Lord Śākyamuni’s eyebrows. Immediately entering samādhi while reciting the mantra, one will obtain fruits of the accumulated
                        great merit and will certainly become destined for awakening. {11.199}

lg.l:
“For those with both inner and outer
                                phyi dang nang la mkhas pa dang / Tib. “Outer” has been supplied
                                from the Tibetan as the corresponding Skt. reading seems corrupt. skills,

One should always
                                ’bras bcas Tib. The Tibetan translation reflects the Sanskrit
                                    *saphalam instead of the extant Skt. sakalam. commence an activity employing [these causes]. {11.254}

tei:div.p:
“Good! May the Blessed One show us
                            bdag la Tib.  the favor of teaching this for the sake of all
                        those who entered the samaya of mantra practice, as well as every other being.” {12.2}

lg.l:
One should go to one’s hut.
                                gnyi ga ’gro Tib. In place of the extant Skt. uḍaya (“hut”), the Tibetan here reflects the Sanskrit *ubhaya (“both”).
                            {12.44}

tei:div.p:
“At the beginning, the practitioner should offer into the fire three oblations of ghee, incanted each time with this
                        heart mantra of fire. For someone engaged in this way in the performance of the pacifying, nourishing, or wrathful activities,
                        there are prescribed three types of firewood, respectively. {13.3}

lg.l:
And mixing it with curds. The Skt. compound dadhyamiśre, possibly corrupt, actually suggests not mixing in the curds. This reading,
                                however, is not supported by the Tibetan.
                            {13.35}

For the rites of enrichment, it should be smokeless. Technically speaking,
                                glowing embers are meant, without smoke or flames.
                            {13.37}

As taught by the sages, The last pāda of this verse and the first of the
                                next have been switched.
                            {14.69}

A bracelet,
                                gdu bu Tib. “Bracelet” has been supplied from the Tibetan (Skt.
                                lacunae). a ring, {14.141}

If this mantra
                                sngags kyi rgyal po Tib. The Tibetan calls it “the king of
                                mantras.” is recited six hundred times. {14.170}

Smeared with honey, curds, and ghee,
                                zho dang mar gyis thams cad bsre/ Tib. In the Skt., “honey” is
                                repeated twice, and “curds” is omitted. “Honey” is is omitted in the Tibetan. mixed together. {14.171}

tei:div.p:
At that time, the bodhisattva Vajrapāṇi, the great being, was present in the midst of the same gathering. Seated, he
                        rose from his seat, circumambulated the Blessed One clockwise three times, and, prostrating at the Blessed One’s feet, said
                        this to him: {15.1}

lg.l:
And become accomplished in stupefying mantras. The Skt. can also be
                                translated as “And can be controlled with stupefying mantras.”
                            {15.80}

And always fond of fine
                                phra ba’i gos la rtag tu dga’/ Tib. “Fine” has been supplied from
                                the Tibetan, filling the lacunae in the Skt. clothes;

Has hair
                                sku ni Tib. The Tibetan translates as, “body” in place of
                                “hair.” as black as antimony powder, {15.122}

And understands the essence of all the tantras;
                                longs spyod che zhing sngags rnams shes/ /sngags rnams kun la de nyid
                                    rig/ Tib. The Tibetan translates as, “Who is endowed with good fortune, is skilled in mantra/ And
                                understands all mantras.”
                            {15.125}

And elbows free of blemish;
                                nyams med Tib. The Skt. kharta, which could not be identified, is translated into the Tibetan as “blemish.”
                            {15.143}

Whose head … the surface of the earth;
                                nang stong ma yin mthil mnyam gnas/ Tib. This pāda is unintelligible
                                in the extant Skt. The Tibetan is also obscure.

Are deep
                                ri mo zab cing D. The Tibetan translates as, “a deep line.”
                            and turn toward the right;

After voiding the bodily fluid at the end of physical passion;
                                gang tshe skyes dbang gtsang ba dang / /rtse mo dmar la yang dag
                                    gnas/ Tib. This half-stanza, again, is not very clear. The Tibetan translates as “Whose penis is clean /
                                And has a red tip.”
                            {15.148}

Jupiter looks upon Earth;
                                phur bus sa de mthong bar ’gyur/ Tib. The translation of this
                                half-stanza is partly based on the Tibetan. The Skt. could also be read as “Jupiter is looked at.”
                            {15.159}

Light-phenomena, and burning thunderbolts;
                                ’gul byed sgra drag dmar ba’i mdog /mnam lcags nyid kyang rab rdung
                                    byed/ Tib. The Tibetan translates as, “When the earth shakes, there are terrifying noises, and [all] is
                                the color red, / Meteors strike,”
                            {15.168}

They may inhabit the gods’ realms, The fourth pāda appears to be missing in
                                the Skt. and the Tib.
                            {15.178}

Are liberated from the ocean of saṃsāra. This line is omitted in the Tib.
                                The preceding line, “All the noble buddhas,” begins the next verse.
                            {15.195}

In explaining one’s virtuous and nonvirtuous character traits;
                                bcas pa’i gza’ ni gtso bo ste/ /dge dang mi dge legs tshul bzhad/ D.
                                This half-stanza has been reconstructed based on the Tibetan (Skt.: lacunae).

And the reciters who pursue various virtuous goals;
                                mchog dang tha ma’i spyod pa dang / /dge ba’i don dang ldan pa dang / /zlos pa
                                    grub pa’i mtshan ma dang / /bsgrub bya sgrub min rnam par brtags/ Tib. The Tibetan translates as, “The
                                distinction of highest and lowest conduct, / Having a viruous aim, / The characteristics of what a mantra reciter
                                accomplishes, / And what should and should not be accomplished. /” 
                            {16.16}

By the past, present,
                                da ltar Tib. “Present” has been supplied from the Tibetan. The Skt.
                                translates as, “celestial”; the syntax, however, is not clear. and future buddhas. {16.28}

‘Glory’ (
                                Śrī
                            ) that is called ‘Gentle’ (Mañju), as having inconceivable qualities. {16.32}

A powerful master who enthralls sentient beings.
                                The exact meaning of this verse is far from clear.
                            {17.32}

tei:div.p:
In the interest of all beings, the blessed Śākyamuni then taught about the action “Action” refers here to their astrological influences. of the grahas. He, the
                        omniscient one, taught everything for the benefit of all mantra reciters and in order to fulfill the aims of the mantra
                        [system]. {24.3}

lg.l:
Will generally seek goodness and truth. This pāda is omitted in the
                                Tib.
                            {24.71}

Or the greater part of Viśākhā, These three nakṣatras (though only a part
                                of the last one) are distributed through the Indian sign of Libra.
                            {24.81}

Together they constitute the sign of the Sea Monster. This Indian sign is
                                divided between Capricorn and Aquarius.
                            {24.97}

And of dark-bluish
                                sngo bzangs mdog tu de dag ’gyur/ D. The Tibetan translation
                                suggests a pale-bluish complexion. complexion. {24.100}

Or tormented by diseases. “Or tormented by diseases” om.
                                Tib.
                            {24.156}

When the best planets are joined The next verse suggests that “joined”
                                    (śliṣṭa) means “dwelling in,” i.e., traversing through.
                            with the lunar days;

Is said to make one kṣaṇa.
                                Skt. 24.198cd, which seems to define one kṣaṇa for the second time, is omitted in the Tib.
                            {24.198}

While eclipsing the sun;
                                gal te nyi ma nyin par ni/ /nub bzhin du ni ’dzin ’gyur dang / D.
                                The last pāda is unclear, but the translation here is supported by the Tibetan.
                            {24.208}

tei:div.p:
At that time, the entire congregation of the buddhas and all the world spheres within the great trichiliocosm, with
                        all the living beings and inanimate objects there, became bathed in the same light. Through the Buddha’s blessing, not a
                        single being suffered pain, and a great light could be seen. {25.11}

lg.l:
Of the lordly victors, possess great power. This pāda is missing from the
                                Tib.
                            {25.18}

tei:div.p:
“One should offer into the fire sesame seeds smeared with curds, honey, and ghee one hundred and eight times, three
                        times a day, for seven days. Then, whatever [deity] mantra one pronounces, that [deity] will come under one’s control. It will
                        carry out whatever task is mentioned and fulfill any command. {25.29}

“If one places the bindi while the arsenic is emitting smoke, one will become invisible. If one wants, one will be
                        invisible even to gods. One can reappear and again disappear in a single instant. One will become the king of all invisible
                        beings and will live for three thousand years. {26.17}

“Similarly, one can magically empower all weapons, such as spears, discuses, arrows, javelins, and so forth, as well
                        as cloth “Cloth” om. Tib. shoes, wooden staffs, and sacred
                        threads, following other procedures. For each of them the accomplishment is threefold. {26.37}

“If one wants to enthrall gods, one should offer one thousand and eight oblations of agalloch sticks smeared with
                        curds, honey, and ghee at the three junctions of the day for twenty-one days. If one offers oblations of rice grains mixed
                        with curds, honey, and ghee, one’s store of food will never diminish. {26.43}

“If one recites the mantra The mantra in question, like in most of
                            this section, must be the uṣṇīṣa king bhrūṁ, also referred to as the cakravartin Uṣṇīṣa, or One Syllable. 100,000 times while facing the sun, one will be
                        free from all one’s nonvirtue. {26.44}

At that time, the blessed Śākyamuni again directed his gaze at the realm of the Pure Abode and the hosts of gods
                        dwelling there, as well as all the buddhas, bodhisattvas, pratyeka­buddhas, and the noble śrāvakas, and once again addressed
                        Mañjuśrī the divine youth: {27.1}

lg.l:
As propagated by the victorious ones. “Propagated/spoken by the victorious
                                ones” om. Tib.
                            {27.7}

To the Buddha’s son We learn from  below that this “Buddha’s son” was
                                Mahā­sthāma­prāpta.

                             of great intelligence, {27.23}

tei:div.p:
“Muṁ.
                            mUM Y, K, N, C; mu D.
                        {27.27}

“[Mahā­sthāma­prāpta,] through the Buddha’s blessing, passed on [this mantra] to Samantabhadra. Bodhisattva
                        Samantabhadra, the great being, passed it on to Mañjuśrī, the divine youth. Mañjuśrī, the divine youth, out of his kindness
                        for all beings and the power of his compassion, then placed this mantra in his heart, in his own body. {27.29}

“In the center, one should paint Tathāgata Amitāyurviniścaya­rājendra, 
                         sitting on a jeweled “Jeweled” om. Tib. throne [supported by] lions and expounding the
                        Dharma. He is surrounded by a halo of light, and has a pure, pink complexion. {27.35}

“At the beginning, one will effect self-protection with a single recitation. A double recitation will protect
                        others. With three recitations, one will effect ‘great protection,’ whereby not even a great bodhisattva established on the
                        tenth level would be able to disturb one, not to mention other beings. {27.46}

lg.l:
Remedies, or yogins;
                                sngags rnams kun dang sman rnams dang / /rnal ’byor ldan la ma dad dang
                                    / D. Although the Tibetan supports “yogins” (yoginām),
                                the original readings might have been “yogas” (yogānām) in the sense
                                of “methods,” as this would fit the context better.
                            {27.62}

tei:div.p:
“One should make an effigy of the desired person out of ‘all fragrances,’ chop up its foot with a sharp,
                        single-bladed weapon, and offer the bits as oblations. If the target is a man, one should chop the right foot; if it is a
                        woman, the left. One will thus enthrall whoever one desires. {28.19}

This concludes the section on the rites that rely on the painting procedure. This sentence is missing from the Tib. The Skt. sentence includes one more word, artari or ārtari, which
                            could not be identified; it seems to qualify “rites.”
                        {28.23}

“One should offer oblations, throughout the entire night, of agalloch sticks smeared with mustard oil, more than
                        half a finger long, onto the smokeless embers of cutch firewood. At sunrise, one will behold Noble Mañjuśrī, who will grant
                        whatever boon one desires, except for hedonistic ones. {29.9}

lg.l:
“Indeed, in the city of Vārāṇasī;
                                de nas rtag tu ku sha’i grong/ Tib. The Tibetan translates as, “in
                                the city of “Kuśi” (i.e., Kuśinagara), reflecting the Sanskrit *kuśipuryāṃ instead of the extant Skt. kāśipuryāṃ.

On the surface of the earth.
                                gzhan dag khro ldan sems kyis ni/ /sa steng dag tu mi bzad pas/ D.
                                In the Tibetan, this verse has only two lines that translate as, “Others [do this] because of their cruel disposition;
                                / Very fearsome, [they seize beings] on the surface of the earth.”
                            {31.5}

In conjunction with the 
                                uṣṇīṣa

                            mudrā. {31.27}

. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . This line of text is missing in both
                                the Skt. and the Tib.
                            {31.35}

Is the same as the one just described;
                                ma mo rnams ni gzi brjid che/ de yi thig ’dir gsungs pa yin/ D. The
                                Tibetan translates as, “The words of the illustrious mātṛs / Are spoken here.”

Are the western languages of Vidiśa and Mālava. The city of Vidiśa and the
                                country of Mālava are “western” in relation to the place where the MMK was probably written.
                            {31.43}

“Ritual activity accomplishes that which has not been accomplished;
                                grub med bsgrub bya’i las rnams med/ Tib. The Tibetan translates as,
                                “There are no ritual activities to be accomplished without accomplishment.”

And consist of just one or two syllables. The meaning of this half-stanza
                                is very unclear. “Accented” is missing from the Tib.
                            {33.8}

That are middling or inferior;
                                Skt. 33.13ab
                                om. Tib.

Since they are mantra adepts. The phrase “mantra adepts” (mantravidaḥ) is missing from the Tib. The Skt. of this pāda is likely to
                                be corrupt, as the pāda is hypermetrical.
                            {33.23}

That it has no function or place. The meaning of the last half-stanza is
                                unclear. The Tibetan is of no help.
                            {33.28}

Will bring out good qualities of the syllable huṁ. This verse is arguably the most obscure in this chapter, and the translation proposed
                                might not convey the original meaning. The Tibetan is of little help.
                            {33.31}

And ten of these is, as expected, one 
                                khaḍga

                            (one hundred billion). {33.55}

Is called 
                                sāgara
                             (one hundred sextillion). {33.59}

“[After] the inconceivable there is the terrible;
                                bsam mi khyab las mi bzad gzugs/ Tib. The translation of this pāda
                                is based on the Tibetan, as the Skt. is unmetrical and seems corrupt.

And beyond
                                de yi ’og Tib. In place of “beyond,” the Tibetan translates as,
                                “below that.” that is the virtuous. {33.71}

Techniques (
                                kriyā
                            ) of mining minerals,

Mudrās become a source of protection. The last two pādas and the next verse
                                are omitted in the Tib.
                            {34.26}

From the sage called Saṃkusuma. His full name is Saṃkusumita Rājendra,
                                first mentioned in .
                            {34.37}

Known as a ‘great mudrā.’ “Great mudrā” seems to be a category that applies
                                only to a limited number in the list of one hundred and eight given here.
                            {35.6}

“The twenty-eighth is the spear (
                                śūla
                            ),

The sixty-seventh the spear (
                                kunta
                            ). There was a mudrā called “spear” above.
                                While the Skt. uses different words to differentiate between them, the English language doesn’t have enough words to
                                use a different one every time (all of them—spear, lance, javelin, etc.—have been used in this list).
                            {35.27}

Is called the salutation. As the Skt. breaks off at
                                this point (to resume again in verse 35.35), the next two verses have been supplied from the Tib.
                            {35.32}

Are in a reverse position resembling a plait, “Plait” om.
                                Tib.
                            {35.52}

This mudrā is called the utpala. The Skt. name for a blue lotus (utpala) has been kept here in order to distinguish this mudrā from the other “lotus” mudrā, the padma mudrā
                                    (padma is a pink lotus).
                            {35.59}

It pacifies all the vighnas. Again, this mudrā and the mudrās that follow
                                are not exactly in the same order as in the list above.
                            {35.71}

Forming the hollow space mudrā. This mudrā was listed
                                in a different order in the original list (starting at 
                                ), where the bell was followed by the noose.

“This completes the first twenty The “peacock seat” was the twentieth mudrā
                                in the enumerative list above, even though the order differed from the order in which the mudrās were presented
                                earlier in this chapter. mudrās;

[This lord of mudrās Because of the lacunae in the Skt., we don’t know who
                                or what it is that is brought into the maṇḍala; we can only guess it is the “lord of the mudrās.” The Tibetan doesn’t
                                specify who or what it is.] accomplishes the rites of homa. {35.142}

Repeated here along with instructions. In the Tib., the first half-stanza
                                of this verse is subsumed in the previous verse, and the second is omitted altogether.
                            {35.200}

With all the fingers outstretched;
                                sor mo thams cad kyis bsgrubs pa/ D. The Tibetan translates as,
                                “Forming this with all of the fingers.”

Call it the resting place of all the buddhas. In the
                                enumerative list above and in the next verse, this mudrā is simply called the “resting place.”
                            {35.234}

This mudrā is called the crescent moon. This mudrā and
                                the next (the “lute”) were mentioned in the enumerative list above in the reverse order.
                            {35.236}

As the couple. This mudrā and the next mudrā, the
                                “hollow space,” appeared in the enumerative list near the beginning of this chapter in the reverse order.
                            {35.242}

This mudrā is called the incense. The last pāda is
                                omitted in the Tib.
                            {35.247}

Should be interlaced. Verses 35.248–35.251 are supplied from the Tibetan,
                                as they are missing from the Skt. version.
                            {35.248}

Or creating a maṇḍala at a specific location. This verse is not very clear;
                                the Tib. offers little help.
                            {35.269}

That mudrā is the 
                                uṣṇīṣa
                            ;

Of the sagacious Mañjughoṣa. This mudrā was called the “root” in the
                                original list above. It is difficult to figure out its exact shape.
                            {35.278}

Of the middle and three other fingers.
                                Skt. 35.279 and 35.280 om. Tib.
                            {35.279}

Constitutes the treasury of the Dharma. The mudrā described in the list
                                above as one that “constitutes the treasury of the Dharma” (at ) was the “Dharma mudrā.” It would be fair to guess that this is the same mudrā.
                            {35.280}

tei:div.p:
“In the same way [one should form the mudrās of] the ears, neck, arms, heart, hands, throat, hips, navel, thighs,
                        calves, feet, eyes, mouth, and tongue. By extending the ten fingers one after the other, one will display the mudrā signs in
                        their respective order. They accomplish their respective activities: {36.12}

“ Oṁ Tejorāśi! You are the uṣṇīṣa of the tathāgatas, invisible on their head! Blaze, blaze! You are one and unique! Rip and tear! Chop and split! Hūṁ hūṁ, sphaṭ sphaṭ, svāhā!
Skt.: oṁ tathāgatoṣṇīṣa • anavalokita­mūrdhni tejorāśi hūṁ jvala jvala eka eka dara vidara cchinda bhinda hūṁ hūṁ sphaṭ sphaṭ svāhā.
mdzub mo de nyid phan tshun mnyam par sbyar la gung mo gnyis sbyar ba ni gzi brjid phung po’i phyag rgya’o/ /’dir sngags ni/ oM ta thA ga toSh+NI ShA ba lo ki te/ mUrt+ti te dzo rA shi hUM dzwA la dzwA la/ d+ha ka d+ha ka /da ra bi da ra/ ts+tshin+da ts+tshin+da/ hUM hUM phaT phaT swA hA/ D. The Tibetan translates as, “Then, interlace the index fingers and join them with the middle fingers. This forms the mudrā of Majestic Heap (Tejorāśi). Its mantra is oṁ tathāgatoṣṇīṣāvalokite mūrtti tejorāśi hūṁ jvāla jvāla dhaka dhaka dara vidara cchinda cchinda hūṁ hūṁ phaṭ phaṭ svāhā.” {37.12}

“If the tips of the same index fingers are brought together
The number F.277.a comes after F.277.b because the sections of text are shifted around in the Tibetan, as indicated in
above. and remain in contact in the space between the fingernail phalanges of the needle-shaped middle fingers, this is the mudrā of Mahācakravartin. If the tips of the same index fingers are brought together and mutually touch below the third phalanx of the needle-shaped middle fingers, forming thus the shape of a circle, this is [another] mudrā of Mahācakravartin. If the tips of the same index fingers are brought together and mutually touch in the space between the third phalanges of the needle-shaped middle fingers, this is [another] mudrā of Mahācakravartin, the lord of mantras. mdzub mo de nyid kyi rtse mo yang dag par bkug la gung mo sbyar ba’i sen mo dang tshigs nand du yang dag par sbyar ba ni ’khor los sgyur ba chen po’i phyag rgya’o/ /mdzub mo de nyid kyi rtse mo gnyis yang dag par bkug la gung mo sbyar zhing ’og tu yang dag par sbyar te/ gdub kor ltar byas pa ni ’khor los sgyur ba chen po’i phyag rgya’o/ /mdzub mo de nyid kyi rtse mo yang dag par bkug la gung mo’i tshigs gsum pa’i nang du yang dag par sbyar ba ni sngags kyi bdag po ’khor los sgyur ba chen po’i phyag rgya ste/. The Tibetan translates as, “When one bends the tips of the index fingers and joins the inner joint to the fingernails of the joined middle fingers, this forms the Mahācakravartin mudrā. When one bends the tips of the index fingers and places them below the middle fingers, forming the shape of a bracelet, this mudrā is [also] known as Mahācakravartin. When one bends the tips of the index fingers and places them inside the third [segment] of the joint of the middle fingers, this is the mudrā of the mantra lord, Mahācakravartin.” {37.16}

lg.l:
And the piśācas and piśācīs;
                                rdzu ’phrul chen po sha za mo/ Tib. In place of “piśācas and
                                piśācīs,” the Tibetan translates as, “powerful piśācīs.”
                            {37.34}

And other celestial
                                lha dang lha min bu mo dang / Tib. The Tibetan translates as,
                                “divine and not divine.” ladies;

tei:div.p:
“If the hands display a gracefully formed añjali gesture, this is the mudrā of all the gods inhabiting the realm of
                        form. The same gesture is also the mudrā of the gods inhabiting the formless realm. The last sentence is omitted in the Tib.
                        {37.61}

lg.l:
No being will be capable of feeling anger. In the Tib., this verse is
                                rendered in prose.
                            {37.65}

tei:div.p:
“When this mudrā is employed with the previously described cakravartin One Syllable, it can accomplish all
                        activities. When [One Syllable] is accomplished, the entire Tathāgata family is accomplished, and all the worldly
                        and supramundane mantras are accomplished. When One Syllable is recited, all the mantras are recited. {37.69}

“One will accomplish all activities by combining the one-syllable heart mantra of Avalokiteśvara with the mudrā of
                        Padmadhara, or by combining the vidyā of Pāṇḍaravāsinī with her mudrā—so also, by combining the mudrā of
                        Vajradhara with his one-syllable heart mantra, or by combining the mudrā of Māmakī with her great vidyā. {37.71}

“The mantra of Aparājitā and the mantra the Dharma wheel are suitable for all activities if used in combination
                        with their corresponding mudrās. In short, they will remove all suffering. Whatever activities they are employed for, they
                        will accomplish all of them. {37.89}

“[This mantra of] the spear of the tathāgatas, when employed with its [namesake] mudrā, can accomplish all
                        activities. It can paralyze all the vighnas, evil beings, and enemies, as well as all the gods. Its qualities are infinite.
                        Whatever activities it is employed for, it will accomplish all of them. {37.90}

“This is the mantra of the bodhisattva Avalokiteśvara. When employed together with the padma mudrā, it will
                        accomplish all activities. When it is recited, all the mantras of the Lotus family are being recited. When it is
                        accomplished, all the mantras of the Lotus family are accomplished. {37.98}

“[This mantra], when combined with the mudrā of Pāṇḍaravāsinī or the padma The Skt. name for a pink lotus (padma) has
                            been kept here in order to distinguish this mudrā from the other “lotus” mudrā, the utpala mudrā (utpala is a blue lotus). mudrā, can accomplish all activities. It also
                        affords protection to those visiting any of the charnel grounds. {37.99}

“Just as the Tathāgata is said to be at the forefront, so is the Tathāgata family said to be at the
                        forefront. Therefore, Mañjuśrī, this king of manuals is the jewel of the most eminent of families. It was taught, elucidated,
                        established, and propagated by the past buddhas since time immemorial. {37.109}

“These eight benefits will manifest for those with faith and no doubts, who earnestly apply themselves to the
                        practice—householders or renunciants, women or men—if they follow the instructions. They will not manifest for others. For
                        those engaging in evil actions the opposite will happen; they will proceed downward into the Raurava and other hells. {37.114}

“As has been said, displaying the mudrās in the forenoon is conducive to long life. One should respectfully display the mudrās of the uṣṇīṣa [kings], and of Locanā
Locanā probably refers to Tathāgata­locanā, the “eye of the tathāgatas” personified. Earlier on
tathāgata­locanā was translated as the “[the mudrā of] the eye of the tathāgatas.” Here, however, as we speak of the deities from the retinue of the Tathāgata, it is more appropriate to use the proper name, Locanā, which is short for Tathāgata­locanā. and the others, while reciting the heart mantras of the deities from the retinue of the Tathāgata. They are of the same efficacy and power as your mudrās and mantras, O divine youth Mañjuśrī. {37.115}

“Applying this procedure, one should rise early, go to the toilet, brush one’s teeth, rinse one’s mouth, and wash
                        oneself, as before, in clean water free of living organisms. Facing the east, one should display the mudrā and recite the
                        mantra. {37.121}

“This king of manuals is filled with infinite sets of mudrās and mantras, This clause is omitted in the Tib. which have been and will be taught by innumerable
                        blessed buddhas. I too—Tathāgata “Tathāgata” om. Tib.
                        Śākyamuni, a worthy, fully realized buddha—have just now taught them in the midst of this great assembly. {37.123}

“You also, Mañjuśrī, divine youth, will engage in the task of safeguarding these instructions and safeguarding the
                        domain of the teachings over the long period of time when I have passed into nirvāṇa, dissolved into the ultimate reality, and
                        attained the complete peace of the final state. {37.124}

lg.l:
One should draw a fruit that is a source of phalaja. The last stanza is omitted in the Tib. The fruit spoken of could be citron, as it is
                                associated with the king of yakṣas Kubera. It is not clear what phalaja (“fruit born”) refers to.
                            {38.20}

tei:div.p:
 The chapter number jumps from 38 to 50 here because the chapters
    from 39 to 49 have been left out as they are missing from the Tibetan translation.At that time Blessed Vajrapāṇi,
the general of the yakṣas who was in the assembly, got up from his seat, draped his
upper robe over the left shoulder, placed his right knee on the ground, bowed
    “Bowed” om. Tib. to the Blessed One with his palms pressed together, and made the following request:
    {50.1}

The Blessed One, remaining silent, entered the samādhi called the buddha’s blessing through miraculous
                            transformation. The divine youth Mañjuśrī, too, remained silent. The entire assembly and its surroundings began
                        to shake in six different ways. {50.4}

lg.l:
Of the Dharma king. “Dharma king” is a frequent epithet of the Buddha
                                Śākyamuni.
                            {50.7}

A samaya connection This process of “forging the samaya” ends more than
                                twenty verses below, when they have heard Mañjughoṣa’s explanation of the actual samaya and its boons. by
                            saying, {50.8}

[Such as rebirth as] a god of Puṇyābha
                                bsod rnams skyes D. The Tibetan reflects the Sanskrit *Puṇyaprasavāṃ instead of the extant Skt. Puṇyābhāṃ. Both are the names of a class of gods and their realm.  or Asaṃjñaka.
                                {50.29}

The [target’s] son will die;
                                dang por bu dag ’chi ba ni/ /sems can zhugs nas de bzhin ’gyur/ Tib.
                                The Tibetan translates as, “His sons will die during the first watch of the night, / As will a being who has just
                                entered [his wife’s womb].”
                            {51.35}

And infinite number
                                brgya dag Tib. The Tibetan translates as, “hundreds.” of pure
                            places. {51.49}

No being can escape him;
                                gzhan gyis nus par mi ’gyur ro/ Tib. The Tibetan translates as,
                                “Others cannot overpower him.”

Salt, the three pungent substances, The “three pungent substances” could be
                                black and long pepper and dry ginger (Monier-Williams).
                            {51.57}

The root of ribbed gourd,
                                Luffa acuntagula.
                            {51.58}

And yakṣiṇīs everywhere. Although the meaning is not quite clear, this
                                verse seems to be referring to the next chapter, where some rites involving yakṣas are described.
                            {51.73}

tei:div.p:
The great being Bodhisattva Vajrapāṇi replied to Bodhisattva Śāntamati, “Śāntamati! A bodhisattva should indeed
                        train to be this way and should adopt such an attitude. What you now say and teach, all the buddhas and the bodhisattvas of
                        great power teach. I will explain it, too, speaking from the perspective of the absolute truth. {52.4}

“Only after forming this wish, Śāntamati, can a bodhisattva who recites the mantra perform a violent rite of
                        assault, applying the skillful means. In all [such] rites, he must be aware of his motive and avoid any unwholesome motives.
                        When undertaking to guide beings, he must strive to do this with a mind full of compassion. {52.10}

Bodhisattva Śāntamati, the great being, looked at the yakṣa general
                        Vajrapāṇi in silence and remained in his seat. Looking then at Lord Buddha, he thought, “The Buddha’s teachings are
                        inconceivable.” {52.13}

Vajrapāṇi, the master of guhyakas, directing his gaze at the entire assembly, continued
                        to teach the ritual of Lord of Wrath: “Listen, respectable hosts of gods and all the hordes of bhūtas who dwell
                        in the inhabited worlds! {52.14}

“Having performed the protection [rite] with care, one should return [to the abandoned house] before dawn. At
                        sunrise, one should wash oneself and, when clean, put on clean clothes and go to one’s own house or a place of one’s choice.
                            {52.24}

“Then, if one takes the ashes with one’s right hand and throws them at the head of any male being, whether
                        two-legged or four-legged—a man, a god, a nāga, or a yakṣa—he will become enthralled. If one takes the ashes with one’s left hand and throws
                        them at the head of any female being, whether human or nonhuman, “Nonhuman” om. Tib. she will become enthralled. {52.25}

“The following is the remedial procedure. One should blend together licorice root, blue lotus, and white sandalwood.
                        One should mash these ingredients together with cool water and smear [the paste on the target’s body], starting from the head
                        and ending with the soles of the feet, while reciting the mantra of Noble Mañjuśrī. The target will become well again. {52.32}

“Similarly, if one throws the ashes with the wind at the enemy forces, even a powerful army will be defeated. It
                        will be seized with a great febrility or fever and its elephants, horses, chariots, banners, and so forth, including the
                        general, will be destroyed or taken prisoner. {52.37}

lg.l:
Can grant every desire. The word used in Skt., kāma, can mean “desire” as well as “sexual pleasure,” and even in the sense of “desire” it
                                has a strong sexual connotation.
                            {52.39}

tei:div.p:
“One should light a fire with sticks of cutch wood and offer into it, at the three junctions of the day for one
                        month, one thousand and eight oblations of beautyberry flowers smeared with ghee. This constitutes the preliminary practice.
                            {52.62}

“Her painting should be executed exactly the same as [the painting of Guhāvāsinī], except for the cave dwelling.
                        Instead, Naravīrā should be drawn next to an aśoka tree. The entire ritual is just like that of Guhāvāsinī, except that
                        Naravīrā should be addressed, ‘Please be my sister!’ {52.68}

“Discarding the myrobalan fruits, one should take the oil and put it in a new vessel made of gold, silver, copper,
                        or clay, and place it on the bed where the feet are. One should incant the oil one thousand times with the one-syllable
                        yakṣiṇī mantra and go to sleep in the same bed, placed in a dark, secluded place and strewn with flowers. {52.76}

“Her practice is as follows. One should build a secluded hut within a garden enclosure, below an aśoka tree. It
                        should be well hidden and have a high wall with a paneled, bolted door. Maintaining purity, one should recite the mantra one
                        hundred thousand times. {52.81}

lg.l:
The mantras of the sagacious Mañjughoṣa, Mañjughoṣa begins the list of male
                                mantra deities in the Skt. but is omitted in the Tib.
                            {52.131}

Of the lord of the yakṣas, The
                                context suggests that the “lord of the yakṣas” is Vajrapāṇi. a very
                            powerful bodhisattva, {52.132}

tei:div.p:
“Homage to all the buddhas who arise out of the absence of the independent existence of entities! Homage to the
                        pratyeka­buddhas and the noble śrāvakas! Homage to the bodhisattvas, the lords established on the ten levels, the great
                        beings! This is the mantra proper: {52.145}

lg.l:
And had root spurs that provided shelter, The holy fig tree has huge root
                                spurs that can provide a cozy shelter.
                            {53.32}

And adorned with the four abodes of Brahmā; “The four abodes of Brahmā,” or
                                the four immeasurable states, are immeasurable loving kindness, compassion, sympathetic joy, and equanimity.
                            {53.43}

With Brahmā and the others, including the Destroyer of Cities;
                                tshangs sogs brgya byin bcas pa dang / Tib. The Tibetan translates
                                as “Brahmā and the rest including Śakra.” However, in this case “Destroyer of Cities” could be an epithet of Śiva, as
                                Indra has already been mentioned.
                            {53.49}

Overwhelmed by grief for his father. Ajātaśatru murdered his own
                                father.
                            {53.131}

The fine city referred to as ‘royal’;
                                ma ga d+hA yi grong mchog tu/ /grong khyer bzang po rgyal po khab/
                                D. The city “referred to as royal” is Rājagṛha. The Tibetan translates as, “To the capital city of Magadhā, / The
                                beautiful city of Rājagṛha.”
                            {53.214}

And bless The verb used for blessing, adhi + √ṣṭhā, could also imply that
                                Mahākāśyapa is restoring Ajātaśatru’s legitimacy (in the moral sense) as a king. him with his magical power.
                                {53.242}

“Be my playmate in the sand.” Literally “player in the dirt” (pāṃsukrīḍanaka), which implies playing in the sand or in direct contact
                                with earth.
                            {53.286}

For the pratyeka­buddhas and the victorious
                                rang rgyal skyob pa dag la ni/ Tib. “Victorious” om.
                                Tib. saviors, {53.307}

And the powerful Vairāṭa. The Sanskrit syntax could also be interpreted as
                                “There is the powerful king Śuddhodana, / Also known as Vairāṭa.”
                            {53.325}

The mantra of Tejorāśi. The list of the mantra deities starting here
                                includes several of the eight uṣṇīṣa kings.
                            {53.359}

The One Syllable mantra. The One Syllable meant here is possibly bhrūṁ, i.e., the mantra of the uṣṇīṣa king simply called Rājoṣṇīṣa or
                                    Uṣṇīṣa.
                            {53.360}

Assuming the form of his mantra deity. It is unclear whether this verse is
                                about King Brahmadatta or the deity Lokeśa.
                            {53.370}

Over the period of the three reigns. The three reigns are those of
                                Candragupta, Bindusāra, and Aśoka.
                            {53.420}

The vidyā

                                de yi rig pa grub pa ’di/ D. “Vidyā” has been supplied
                                from the Tibetan. called Śāladūtī. {53.453}

But will not find
                                kun tu yang ni ma mthong gyur/ D. The Skt. form dāsyante is a bit of a mystery. Here it has been translated as “will
                                [not] find” based on the Tibetan translation. Possibly, it should be emended to drakṣyante. it anywhere. {53.468}

The holder of the vajra, This seems to refer to Vajrapāṇi, the father of
                                Mūrdhaṭaka (cf. -39).
                            {53.469}

Of the divine youth of great splendor “The divine youth of great splendor”
                                seems to refer here to Mañjuśrī.
                            {53.511}

Taught by the Dharma King. “Dharma King” most likely refers here to the
                                buddha Śākyamuni.
                            {53.563}

And Mādhava, Pradyumna. The translation of this verse follows the
                                interpretation found in Jayaswal 1934, p. 30.
                            {53.567}

Because of grief for his son. This half-stanza is missing from the
                                Tib.
                            {53.592}

The king of gods
                                der ni lha rgyal zhes byar ’gyur/ Tib. The Tibetan translates as,
                                “He will be known as Devarāja.” Devarāja means “king of gods.” destined for awakening. {53.594}

But ever careful and vigilant about his own person. Jayaswal takes the
                                description in this and the following verses to apply not to Bhasma, but to Samudra.
                            {53.638}

As far as the banks of the Daśānūpa. 53.757–759 are translated from the
                                Tibetan, as they are omitted in the Skt.
                            {53.756}

Toward awakening. This half-stanza, about him progressing toward awakening,
                                is somewhat at odds with the epithets used earlier—jina, sugata, and cakravartin—that suggest that he had already attained the highest accomplishment.
                            {53.768}

Begins with Y. This king has been identified by
                                Jayaswal as Yaśovarman of the early eighth century.
                            {53.787}

And should be employed at three different times. The “three times” possibly
                                refers to the three times of the day (or night) that are said to be most suitable for performing rites.
                            {53.848}

The doctrines of other systems, de nas yi ge sa dang ka /dang po dag tu brjod pa dang / /de bzhin yi ge ra dang g+ha/ /dang po dag tu rab brjod dang / dang por yi ge ba dang ni/ /legs byin legs de sde zhes bstan/ /sbyin byed byin par byed pa nyid/ /gzhan gyi grub mtha’ sun byed pa/ D. The Tibetan that corresponds to Skt. 53.870cd–871 differs in its content, renders this material in eight verses instead of six, and includes one verse with additional content. The Tibetan translates as, “Then there will be the ones known / By the first initials S and K, and / Likewise the ones known / By the first initials R and Gh, / The ones with the first initials V and *Ni, / Sudatta and **Suṣeṇa who is known as Sena, / And Dattaka and Dāyaka / Who will refute the doctrinal systems of others.”
*This ni is assumed to represent the first initial of a name and not the usual nominative particle, following the pattern already established in this section of verses. It is also quite possible that this is a scribal error for na that has been mistakenly rendered to look like a nominative particle ni in the Tibetan.
** The Tibetan translation of this line is problematic and does not resolve the issue with the Skt. rendering of this line mentioned above. {53.871}

He will recite the six-syllable mantra, The six-syllable mantra is probably
                                    oṁ vākyeda namaḥ, the main heart mantra of Mañjuśrī.
                            {53.879}

The long-lived
                                nges pa’i tsher ni Y, K; nges pa’i tshar ni N; nges pa’i
                                    tshig ni D.  among the gods. {53.901}

Is the majestic Citraketu. The Skt. actually reads vidyācitraketu, which is hypermetrical and unlikely to be a name.
                                Citraketu, the king of vidyādharas, was also mentioned in verse  above.
                            {53.908}

That extend from the east to the west. The Skt. of this verse has been
                                heavily emended to make some sense and the translation should be regarded as tentative.
                            {53.914}

Where there are kings are said to be infinite. This verse, again, is very
                                unclear and the meaning has been half guessed.
                            {53.915}

By them Śākyamuni is using here the plural number referring to himself, or
                                perhaps he includes in the statement also Mañjuśrī and Vajrapāṇi who likewise contributed to the discourse presented
                                in the MMK. above the realm of the Pure Abode.” {53.919}

Rākṣasas, or piśācas there are, This verse seems to be missing one pāda in
                                both the Skt. and the Tib.
                            {54.16}

tei:div.p:
“As soon as this mantra was pronounced by the great being, the divine youth Mañjuśrī, the earth with its mountains
                        and oceans, up to its farthest reaches, shook in six different ways, and so did all the infinite buddhafields and world
                        spheres up to their farthest limits. All the blessed buddhas empowered the words of the mantra. {54.65}

lg.l:
Becoming ever more feebleminded. This verse is missing from the Tib., where
                                some parts of it appear in the next verse.
                            {54.94}

